---
// BACK-PAIN TRAINING PAGE (doc 74 §1). Long-tail phrase "personal trainer for
// back pain"; non-geo title, Denver in the body. Trainer first: the rehab
// detail lives on /what-i-work-with/chronic-back-pain and is linked, not
// restated.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import StickyBook from '../../components/StickyBook.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';
import { REVIEWS, REVIEWS_URL } from '../../data/reviews';
import { testimonialCount } from '../../data/videos';

import hero from '../../assets/training/crutch-overhead.jpg';

const title = 'Personal Trainer for Back Pain | Train Hard, Back Included';
const description =
  'Personal training in downtown Denver for people with a back that has a history. Cognitive Functional Therapy tells me what your back will accept; then we train hard. $57 assessment, studio or Zoom.';

const adrienne = REVIEWS.find(r => r.name === 'Adrienne Rule');
const backVideos = testimonialCount('chronic-back-pain');

const faq = [
  { q: 'Should I lift with a disc bulge?', a: 'Usually yes, with someone reading the load. Disc bulges show up on the scans of a great many people with no pain at all; the assessment, not the picture, decides what your back will accept this week.' },
  { q: 'Do I need a scan first?', a: 'No, unless your doctor has asked for one. Scans describe, they don\'t decide. A small number of presentations do need a physician first, and I screen for those in the first thirty minutes.' },
  { q: 'Will you make me do deadlifts?', a: 'Eventually, in whatever shape your back accepts. Nobody starts there. We start with the hinge your back already tolerates and raise the load from there.' },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map(f => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
};

const serviceSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Personal training for back pain',
  serviceType: 'Personal training',
  provider: { '@id': SITE.url },
  areaServed: { '@type': 'City', name: 'Denver' },
  url: `${SITE.url}/personal-trainer-denver/back-pain`,
  offers: { '@type': 'Offer', name: '30-minute assessment (first visit)', price: '57', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} image="/studio-coaching.jpg">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(serviceSchema)} />

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="px-6 pt-14 pb-10 md:pt-20">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto grid md:grid-cols-[1.1fr_.9fr] gap-10 items-center">
      <div>
        <p class="font-serif italic text-[var(--color-primary)] mb-3 text-lg">Training with a bad back</p>
        <h1 class="mb-6">Personal training for people whose back has an opinion</h1>
        <p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
          Most trainers have two settings for a bad back. Avoid it, or ignore it. Both make the back worse over a
          year, for opposite reasons.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
          <a href="/book" class="cta-button text-lg">Book a 30-minute assessment</a>
          <span class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">{SITE.pricing.firstVisit}, in the studio or on Zoom</span>
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-primary)]">
          {SITE.awardBadge} · 9NEWS fitness expert · {SITE.contact.address.street}, downtown Denver
        </p>
      </div>
      <Image
        src={hero}
        alt="A client holding his crutch overhead at Jamie Atlas's downtown Denver studio, training after a back injury"
        widths={[640, 1000]}
        sizes="(min-width: 768px) 45vw, 100vw"
        format="webp"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
        class="rounded-xl border border-[var(--color-border)] w-full h-auto max-h-[560px] object-cover object-top"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BODY -->
  <section class="px-6 py-12 bg-[var(--color-surface)] border-y border-[var(--color-border)]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-sm font-bold tracking-[0.12em] uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)] mb-2">The problem with "just be careful"</h2>
      <p>
        You've been told to protect it. Brace, avoid bending, skip the deadlifts, sit up straight. So you did, and the
        back got weaker along with the rest of you, and now a bag of groceries is a decision. That's not a moral
        failing. A back that is never loaded loses the capacity to be loaded, and a nervous system that is told the
        back is fragile starts treating ordinary movement as a threat.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-text)] font-semibold text-xl">Backs don't get better by being spared. They get better by being convinced.</p>

      <h2 class="text-sm font-bold tracking-[0.12em] uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)] mb-2 pt-6">What I do differently</h2>
      <p>
        I'm a personal trainer with a rehab specialist's training in what a back will accept. Cognitive Functional
        Therapy, trained under Peter O'Sullivan, is how I read your pain system: which movements it's guarding, which
        of those it will let go of today, and which need to wait. Applied Functional Science, from the Gray Institute,
        is how I decide where the load goes so the hips and the mid-back do their share instead of leaving it all to
        the sore bit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then we train. Properly. Hinges, carries, squats, presses, at loads that go up. The back is in the program,
        not around it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-sm font-bold tracking-[0.12em] uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)] mb-2 pt-6">Who this is for</h2>
      <p>
        The disc bulge that "showed something" on the MRI and nothing since. The back that goes once a year when you
        sneeze. The one that's fine until the third hour on the mountain. The one that's been through the PT, the
        chiro and the injection and is still the reason you don't lift. If you've been cleared, or nobody has ever
        found anything to clear you of, this page is you.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is not for you today if the pain came with numbness in both legs, bladder or bowel changes, fever, or a
        fall. Those need a doctor first, and I'll say so in the assessment.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-sm font-bold tracking-[0.12em] uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)] mb-2 pt-6">How a session runs</h2>
      <p>
        First visit: a 30-minute assessment, {SITE.pricing.firstVisit}. I watch you bend, hinge and load, find the one
        change that helps most, and you feel it before you leave. You get a plain read on whether I can help.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then 60 minutes, or 30, one-on-one in the studio at {SITE.contact.address.street} in downtown Denver or on Zoom.
        We warm up the back with the movements it already tolerates, train hard everywhere else, and every few weeks go
        back and load the thing you were worried about to see whether it's still worth worrying about. Nutrition
        coaching is included; it matters more for backs than people expect, because a body that isn't recovering
        doesn't tolerate load.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-sm font-bold tracking-[0.12em] uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)] mb-2 pt-6">What clients on camera say</h2>
      <p>
        {backVideos} of the 23 clients who let me film their sessions came in for a back. Each rates their pain before
        and after, on camera. Results vary from person to person.
        <a href="/testimonials#chronic-back-pain">Watch the back videos →</a>
      </p>

      {adrienne && (
        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[var(--color-primary)] pl-5 py-1 text-base italic">
          "{adrienne.text}"
          <footer class="not-italic text-sm mt-2 font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">{adrienne.name}, <a href={REVIEWS_URL} rel="noopener">on Google</a></footer>
        </blockquote>
      )}

      <p class="text-[var(--color-text)] font-semibold pt-4">
        Point is this: the goal isn't a back that never hurts. It's a back you stop planning your life around.
      </p>

      <p class="text-base pt-2">
        <a href="/what-i-work-with/chronic-back-pain">The rehab side of back pain, in detail →</a> ·
        <a href="/what-i-work-with/sciatica">Sciatica →</a> ·
        <a href="/resources/why-pain-flares-some-weeks">Why pain flares some weeks →</a> ·
        <a href="/personal-trainer-denver">Personal training in downtown Denver →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="px-6 py-14">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="mb-6">Questions people with a bad back ask</h2>
      <div>
        {faq.map((f, i) => (
          <details open={i === 0} class="border-t border-[var(--color-border)] py-4 last:border-b">
            <summary class="font-serif font-bold text-xl cursor-pointer">{f.q}</summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FINAL CTA -->
  <section class="px-6 py-20 bg-[var(--color-primary)] text-on-dark">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
      <h2 class="mb-6 text-white">Thirty minutes. One measurable change. Then you decide.</h2>
      <p class="text-xl mb-10 opacity-95">
        A 30-minute assessment, {SITE.pricing.firstVisit}, in the studio or on Zoom. If it's not a fit I'll say so in the
        first half hour, and I'll know who is.
      </p>
      <a href="/book" class="cta-button cta-button-accent text-lg">Book a 30-minute assessment</a>
      <p class="mt-8 text-sm opacity-80">
        {SITE.contact.address.street}, {SITE.contact.address.level} · <a href={`tel:${SITE.contact.phoneTel}`} class="text-white underline">{SITE.contact.phone}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <StickyBook />
</BaseLayout>
